omeone is red, then they are not green**: Since Gary is red, according to this rule, Gary cannot be green.\n- **If someone is green, they are not nice**: Since Gary is green, Gary cannot be nice.\n- **Nice people are not young**: Since Gary is nice, Gary cannot be young.\n- **If someone is white, they are not young**: Since Gary is not white (he is red), this rule applies to Gary.\n- **If someone is cold, they are not white**: Since Gary is cold, this rule applies to Gary.\n- **If someone is green and smart, they are not white**: Since Gary is green, this rule applies to Gary because we don\'t know if Gary is smart.\n- **All young, red people are not smart**: Since Gary is young and red, this rule applies to Gary because we don\'t know if Gary is smart.\n- **If Fiona is red, then Fiona is not green**: Since Gary is not red, this rule applies to Gary.\n- **If Anne is smart, then Anne is green**: Since Gary is not mentioned in relation to Anne, this rule applies to Gary.\n\nBased on the analysis, we can conclude that Gary is not green. Since being green implies being nice, and nice people are not young, Gary must not be young. However, the statement "Gary is not smart" can be definitively determined from the given information because we have enough information about Gary\'s smartness. Therefore, the statement "Gary is not smart" is known.\n\n<answer>\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
